            <h1>Global Smartwatch Secondary Market Analysis</h1>
            <p>Q1 2026 • 3,607 listings across Apple, Garmin, and Samsung</p>
            <div class="meta">Report for E-Commerce Strategy Analysts | Data Source: Kaggle Marketplace Insights</div>
        </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container">
        <!-- Executive Summary KPIs -->
        <div class="kpi-grid">
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <h3>Total Listings Analyzed</h3>
                <div class="value">3,607</div>
                <div class="sub">Across 18 countries, 1,754 sellers</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <h3>Apple Median Price</h3>
                <div class="value">$550</div>
                <div class="sub">2,370 listings | Range: $120–$40,500</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <h3>Garmin Median Price</h3>
                <div class="value">$567</div>
                <div class="sub">746 listings | Range: $215–$1,319</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <h3>Samsung Median Price</h3>
                <div class="value">$530</div>
                <div class="sub">470 listings | Range: $245–$49,900</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: Market Overview -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Market Overview & Listing Composition</h2>
            <p class="section-intro">The secondary smartwatch market demonstrates clear brand concentration, with Apple commanding 66% of all resale listings, followed by Garmin (21%) and Samsung (13%). Condition grading reveals a balanced mix between new and used inventory.</p>

            <div class="two-col">
                <div>
                    <div class="chart-title">Listing Distribution by Brand</div>
                    <div class="bar-chart" id="brand-distribution"></div>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <div class="chart-title">Condition Mix Across Brands</div>
                    <table id="condition-table">
                        <thead><tr><th>Brand</th><th>New</th><th>Used</th><th>Open Box</th><th>Refurbished</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody></tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Key Finding:</strong> Apple listings are heavily concentrated in the United States (1,831 of 2,370), while Garmin and Samsung show greater geographic diversity. The US market skews toward Apple at 78% of all US listings.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 2: Brand Pricing Analysis -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Brand-Level Pricing & Value Retention</h2>
            <p class="section-intro">While Apple dominates volume, Garmin commands the highest median resale price at $567. Samsung shows the widest price variance (std: $2,286) driven by premium Galaxy Watch models reaching up to $49,900.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <div class="chart-title">Median Price Comparison by Brand (USD)</div>
                <div id="brand-price-chart"></div>
            </div>

            <div class="kpi-grid" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
                <div class="kpi-card" style="border-left-color: var(--secondary);">
                    <h3>Apple Price Std Dev</h3>
                    <div class="value">$834</div>
                    <div class="sub">High variance indicates Apple Watch diversity</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card" style="border-left-color: var(--success);">
                    <h3>Garmin Price Std Dev</h3>
                    <div class="value">$198</div>
                    <div class="sub">Most consistent pricing across listings</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card" style="border-left-color: var(--danger);">
                    <h3>Samsung Price Std Dev</h3>
                    <div class="value">$2,286</div>
                    <div class="sub">Extreme outliers skew average upward</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Value Retention Insight:</strong> Garmin watches demonstrate the strongest price consistency (lowest std dev relative to median), suggesting they hold resale value predictably. Apple's high variance reflects premium Series 9/Ultra models alongside older Series generations.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 3: Geographic Pricing Arbitrage -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Geographic Pricing Arbitrage</h2>
            <p class="section-intro">Cross-border price differentials reveal actionable arbitrage opportunities. Geographic spreads of $384–$399 exist for Apple and Garmin, while Samsung's spread is extreme due to outlier listings in the US market.</p>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <h3>Apple Geographic Spread</h3>
                    <div class="value">$399</div>
                    <div class="sub">Australia ($792) vs UK ($393)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <h3>Garmin Geographic Spread</h3>
                    <div class="value">$384</div>
                    <div class="sub">Canada ($890) vs Japan ($506)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <h3>Samsung Geographic Spread</h3>
                    <div class="value">$29,850*</div>
                    <div class="sub">*Outlier-adjusted: ~$345</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <div class="chart-title">Apple Watch Median Price by Country (USD)</div>
                <div id="apple-geo-chart"></div>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <div class="chart-title">Garmin Median Price by Country (USD)</div>
                <div id="garmin-geo-chart"></div>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <div class="chart-title">Samsung Median Price by Country (USD)</div>
                <div id="samsung-geo-chart"></div>
            </div>

            <div class="actionable">
                <h4>Arbitrage Opportunities by Region</h4>
                <p><strong>Buy Low:</strong> United Kingdom (Apple: $393, Garmin: $775) and Japan (Garmin: $506) offer the lowest entry points for secondary-market sourcing.</p>
                <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Sell High:</strong> Australia commands premium pricing across all brands (Apple: $792, Garmin: $1,005). Canada shows elevated Garmin pricing ($890).</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 4: Case Size Premium -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Case Size & Price Premium Analysis</h2>
            <p class="section-intro">Physical dimensions correlate weakly with resale value across all brands. Case size explains less than 12% of price variance in any single brand segment.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <div id="case-size-analysis"></div>
            </div>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <h3>Apple Case Size Correlation</h3>
                    <div class="value">r = 0.033</div>
                    <div class="sub">2,034 listings | Virtually no correlation</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <h3>Garmin Case Size Correlation</h3>
                    <div class="value">r = 0.107</div>
                    <div class="sub">306 listings | Weak positive correlation</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <h3>Samsung Case Size Correlation</h3>
                    <div class="value">r = 0.035</div>
                    <div class="sub">384 listings | Negligible correlation</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Strategic Implication:</strong> Case size is not a reliable premium predictor in the secondary market. Buyers and sellers should focus on brand, condition, and model generation rather than physical dimensions when pricing listings.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 5: Power Seller Behavior -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Power Seller Behavior & Pricing Patterns</h2>
            <p class="section-intro">The secondary smartwatch market exhibits high concentration: the top 5% of sellers by volume control nearly half (48.4%) of all listings. This asymmetry creates both partnership opportunities and pricing leverage dynamics.</p>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <h3>Top 5% Seller Concentration</h3>
                    <div class="value">48.4%</div>
                    <div class="sub">87 sellers control 1,745 listings</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <h3>Power Seller Median Price</h3>
                    <div class="value">$598</div>
                    <div class="sub">vs Regular seller: $520</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <h3>Power Seller Premium</h3>
                    <div class="value">+15%</div>
                    <div class="sub">Above regular seller median</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="two-col" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
                <div>
                    <div class="chart-title">Pricing Distribution: Power vs Regular Sellers</div>
                    <div id="seller-comparison-chart"></div>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <div class="chart-title">Top 10 Power Sellers by Volume</div>
                    <table id="top-sellers-table">
                        <thead><tr><th>Seller ID</th><th>Listings</th><th>Median Price</th><th>Top Brand</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody></tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Power Seller Strategy:</strong> High-volume sellers price 15% above market median, likely due to professional grading, better condition descriptions, and established seller ratings. Partnering with power sellers for inventory access or adopting their listing standards could improve resale performance.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 6: Strategic Recommendations -->
        <div class="section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a365d 0%, #2c5282 100%); color: white;">
            <h2 style="border-bottom-color: var(--accent); color: white;">Strategic Recommendations</h2>
            <p class="section-intro" style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);">Based on data analysis of 3,607 listings, the following recommendations address sourcing, pricing, and partnership decisions for secondary-market operators.</p>

            <div class="recommendations-grid" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
                <div class="rec-card">
                    <h4>1. Target Garmin for Consistent Margins</h4>
                    <p>Garmin's low price variance (std: $198) and strong median ($567) make it ideal for predictable margin extraction. Source from Japan (median: $506) and sell into Canada/Australia where premiums exceed $400.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="rec-card">
                    <h4>2. Exploit UK-to-Australia Arbitrage for Apple</h4>
                    <p>Apple Watch pricing in UK ($393 median) vs Australia ($792) creates a $399 spread. Acquire from UK sellers, leverage Australia's elevated demand, and account for shipping costs in margin calculations.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="rec-card">
                    <h4>3. Prioritize New Condition Apple Inventory</h4>
                    <p>Apple's New vs Used premium of 17.7% is the highest across all brands. Stocking New condition Apple Watch units offers 3× the premium uplift versus Garmin (0%) and Samsung (0.6%).</p>
                </div>
                <div class="rec-card">
                    <h4>4. Partner with Power Sellers for Volume</h4>
                    <p>The top 87 sellers control 48.4% of inventory at 15% price premium. Negotiate bulk acquisition agreements or become a power seller yourself through consistent listing quality and inventory depth.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="rec-card">
                    <h4>5. Ignore Case Size in Pricing Decisions</h4>
                    <p>Case size shows r² &lt; 1.2% across all brands. Physical dimension data in listings is unreliable and not a price driver. Focus on model generation and condition grade instead.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="rec-card">
                    <h4>6. Leverage Worldwide Shipping Sellers</h4>
                    <p>Listings with worldwide shipping command a median premium of $146 for Apple ($676 vs $530). Cross-border fulfillment capability justifies higher prices and expands buyer pool.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <footer style="text-align: center; padding: 2rem; color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 0.875rem;">
            <p>Global Smartwatch Secondary Market Analysis — Q1 2026</p>
            <p>Data: 3,607 listings | Sources: Kaggle Marketplace Insights | Generated for E-Commerce Strategy Analysts</p>
        </footer>
    </div>

